* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
* PARSE(string, pattern, delimiter)
*
* Matches STRING against PATTERN and returns STRING with the delimiter between the part that matched each element of the pattern,
* or a null string if it does not match.  The delimiter is a field mark if it is null, and only its first character is used.
* A pattern may be several templates, separated by value marks: the first that matches is used.
*
* The matching is MATCHES and the parts are those of MATCHFIELD(), so the elements are as the string matching documentation has
* them: n, n-m, 0 with A, N and X, a quoted string, and a tilde in front of an element for its opposite.  The three dots of the
* openQM manual are the same as 0X.  An element of the pattern that is not one of these stands for itself, one character at a
* time, as the manual says of text that is not in quotes.
*
* INMAT() is not set by PARSE here: it is a program's own state and this function is another program.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

function !parsepattern(s, pattern, delim)
$catalog !parsepattern

   d = if delim = '' then @fm else delim[1,1]

   for alt.no = 1 to dcount(pattern, @vm)
      alt = pattern<1,alt.no>
      gosub split.pattern
      if s matches pat then
         out = ''
         for k = 1 to ntok
            if k > 1 then out := d
            out := matchfield(s, pat, k)
         next k
         return out
      end
   next alt.no

   return ''

* The template ALT cut into its elements: PAT is the template with the three dots written as 0X, NTOK the number of elements
split.pattern:
   pat = ''
   ntok = 0
   p = 1
   plen = len(alt)
   loop
   while p <= plen
      start = p
      if alt[p,1] = '~' then p += 1
      c = alt[p,1]
      begin case
         case c = '"' or c = "'"
            e = index(alt[p + 1, plen], c, 1)       ;* the closing quote, or the end of the template if there is none
            if e = 0 then p = plen + 1 else p = p + 1 + e
            tok = alt[start, p - start]
         case alt[p,3] = '...'
            tok = '0X'
            p += 3
         case c matches '1N'
            loop
            while alt[p,1] matches '1N'
               p += 1
            repeat
            if alt[p,1] = '-' then
               p += 1
               loop
               while alt[p,1] matches '1N'
                  p += 1
               repeat
            end
            p += 1                                  ;* the A, N or X
            tok = alt[start, p - start]
         case 1
            p += 1
            tok = alt[start, p - start]
      end case
      pat := tok
      ntok += 1
   repeat
   return
end

